{"id":"beats-725327","type":"UPDATE","payload":{"description":"## Problem\n\nAgents currently pass long markdown descriptions and comments via the `--desc` flag and `beats comment \u003cid\u003e \"\u003ctext\u003e\"` positional argument. Multi-line markdown with backticks, quotes, `$`, and other shell metacharacters frequently breaks under shell escaping, leading to malformed content or aborted commands.\n\n## Proposal\n\nAccept body text from stdin using existing unix conventions — no new flags for the common case:\n\n1. **Auto-detect piped stdin.** If stdin is not a TTY and the relevant text flag was not passed, read the body from stdin:\n   - `echo \"body\" | beats comment \u003cid\u003e`\n   - `cat desc.md | beats add \"title\"`\n   - `cat desc.md | beats update \u003cid\u003e` (defaults to `--desc`)\n\n2. **`-` as explicit opt-in.** Standard unix convention for \"read this argument from stdin\":\n   - `beats add --desc - \"title\" \u003c desc.md`\n   - `beats comment \u003cid\u003e - \u003c body.md`\n   - `beats update \u003cid\u003e --desc - \u003c desc.md`\n\n3. **No `--file` flag.** Shell redirection (`\u003c body.md`) already handles file input; a dedicated flag is redundant.\n\nThe existing `--desc \"string\"` and positional comment forms remain unchanged for short, single-line text.\n\n## Disambiguation for `beats update`\n\n`update` has multiple text fields, but only `--desc` is body-like today. Default behavior: piped stdin with no explicit flag → `--desc`. If a future text field is added (e.g. acceptance criteria), require `--desc -` / `--field -` for explicitness.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] `beats add \"title\"` reads description from stdin when stdin is piped and `--desc` not passed\n- [ ] `beats comment \u003cid\u003e` reads body from stdin when stdin is piped and no positional body passed\n- [ ] `beats update \u003cid\u003e` reads description from stdin when stdin is piped and `--desc` not passed\n- [ ] `--desc -` explicitly opts in to reading from stdin regardless of pipe detection\n- [ ] `beats comment \u003cid\u003e -` explicitly opts in for comment body\n- [ ] Trailing newline trimmed; otherwise content preserved verbatim\n- [ ] No-op (or clear error) if stdin is a TTY and `-` is passed — don't hang waiting for input without a clear indicator\n- [ ] Tests cover multi-line markdown with backticks, quotes, `$`, and `!`\n\n## Rationale\n\nShort-term fix for the shell-escaping pain described in [[beats-7ec710]]. Uses existing unix conventions (pipe detection, `-` for stdin) instead of inventing new flags. Works with any agent transport (CLI today, also useful for humans piping from editors). Does not block the longer-term MCP work in [[beats-5b37e3]]."},"created_at":"2026-05-27T06:31:17.948005254Z","created_by":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e"}
{"id":"beats-725327","type":"UPDATE","payload":{"description":"## Problem\n\nAgents currently pass long markdown descriptions and comments via the `--desc` flag and `beats comment \u003cid\u003e \"\u003ctext\u003e\"` positional argument. Multi-line markdown with backticks, quotes, `$`, and other shell metacharacters frequently breaks under shell escaping, leading to malformed content or aborted commands. Additionally, creating a fully-formed issue today requires multiple commands (`add` → `start`/`planned` → `link`), which compounds the escaping problem and is verbose for agents.\n\n## Proposal\n\nTwo complementary modes:\n\n### Mode 1: Plain-text stdin (body fields)\n\nAccept body text from stdin using existing unix conventions — no new flags for the common case:\n\n1. **Auto-detect piped stdin.** If stdin is not a TTY and the relevant text flag was not passed, read the body from stdin:\n   - `echo \"body\" | beats comment \u003cid\u003e`\n   - `cat desc.md | beats add \"title\"`\n   - `cat desc.md | beats update \u003cid\u003e` (defaults to `--desc`)\n\n2. **`-` as explicit opt-in.** Standard unix convention for \"read this argument from stdin\":\n   - `beats add --desc - \"title\" \u003c desc.md`\n   - `beats comment \u003cid\u003e - \u003c body.md`\n   - `beats update \u003cid\u003e --desc - \u003c desc.md`\n\n3. **No `--file` flag.** Shell redirection (`\u003c body.md`) already handles file input; a dedicated flag is redundant.\n\nThe existing `--desc \"string\"` and positional comment forms remain unchanged for short, single-line text.\n\n### Mode 2: Structured JSON payload\n\nExplicit opt-in via `--json` for full-issue creation/update in a single call:\n\n- `beats add --json - \u003c payload.json` — create a fully-formed issue\n- `beats update \u003cid\u003e --json - \u003c patch.json` — patch any subset of fields\n- `beats comment \u003cid\u003e --json - \u003c comment.json` — comment with structured metadata\n\n**Payload shape for `add`:**\n\n```json\n{\n  \"title\": \"string\",\n  \"description\": \"string (markdown)\",\n  \"labels\": [\"feature\", \"CLI\"],\n  \"parent\": \"beats-7ec710\",\n  \"story_points\": 5,\n  \"assignee\": \"string\",\n  \"status\": \"BACKLOG | PLANNED | DOING | BLOCKED | DONE\",\n  \"links\": [\n    { \"target\": \"beats-7ec710\", \"type\": \"relates_to\" }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nFor `update`, all fields are optional and act as a patch (only provided fields change). For `comment`, the shape is `{ \"body\": \"string\", \"author\": \"string?\" }`.\n\nCrucially, the JSON form is a **superset** of what the flags expose — it can set `status` and `links` at creation time, eliminating the need for a separate `beats planned`/`beats link` follow-up.\n\n## Shared internal function (load-bearing constraint)\n\nThe flag-based path and the `--json` path MUST funnel through the same internal Go function (e.g. `service.CreateIssue(payload IssuePayload)` / `service.UpdateIssue(id, patch IssuePatch)`). This means:\n\n- One source of truth for validation, defaults, and side effects.\n- The future MCP server in [[beats-5b37e3]] calls the same function — no duplicated business logic across transports.\n- Flags become a thin shim that constructs a payload from CLI args.\n\nThis is the actual architectural deliverable; stdin parsing is incidental.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n**Plain-text mode:**\n\n- [ ] `beats add \"title\"` reads description from stdin when stdin is piped and `--desc` not passed\n- [ ] `beats comment \u003cid\u003e` reads body from stdin when stdin is piped and no positional body passed\n- [ ] `beats update \u003cid\u003e` reads description from stdin when stdin is piped and `--desc` not passed\n- [ ] `--desc -` and `beats comment \u003cid\u003e -` explicitly opt in regardless of pipe detection\n- [ ] Trailing newline trimmed; otherwise content preserved verbatim\n- [ ] Clear error (not a hang) if `-` is passed and stdin is a TTY\n\n**JSON mode:**\n\n- [ ] `beats add --json -` accepts the payload shape above and creates a fully-formed issue including status and links in one call\n- [ ] `beats update \u003cid\u003e --json -` applies a partial patch\n- [ ] `beats comment \u003cid\u003e --json -` accepts `{ body, author? }`\n- [ ] Unknown JSON fields produce a clear error (not silently ignored)\n- [ ] Validation errors point to the offending field\n\n**Architectural:**\n\n- [ ] Flag-based `beats add` and `beats add --json -` both call the same internal `CreateIssue` function\n- [ ] Same for `update` and `comment`\n- [ ] Function signatures are designed so the MCP server in [[beats-5b37e3]] can call them directly without further refactoring\n\n**Tests:**\n\n- [ ] Multi-line markdown with backticks, quotes, `$`, and `!` round-trips correctly via plain stdin\n- [ ] JSON payload with all fields set produces an issue matching the payload exactly\n- [ ] Partial JSON patch on `update` leaves untouched fields unchanged\n\n## Rationale\n\nFixes the shell-escaping pain from [[beats-7ec710]] for the common case (plain stdin), and gives agents a single-call structured API for the complex case (JSON). The shared-internal-function constraint means [[beats-5b37e3]] becomes just protocol plumbing on top of an already-structured core, rather than a parallel re-implementation."},"created_at":"2026-05-27T06:42:31.69197571Z","created_by":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e"}
{"id":"beats-5b37e3","type":"UPDATE","payload":{"description":"## Problem\n\nAgents interact with beats through the CLI, which means every argument is squeezed through shell quoting. This causes recurring escaping breakage (see [[beats-7ec710]]) and makes integration with Claude Code, Cursor, and other MCP-capable agents clunkier than it needs to be. Even with stdin support from [[beats-725327]], MCP is the right long-term answer because it gives agents structured tool calls with typed parameters natively, no shell involved at all.\n\n## Dependency on beats-725327\n\nThis ticket builds directly on the structured-core work in [[beats-725327]]. That ticket establishes the internal Go functions (`CreateIssue`, `UpdateIssue`, `AddComment`, etc.) that both the CLI flag-path and the CLI JSON-path call. **MCP tools are a third transport on top of the same functions** — no parallel business logic, no shelling out.\n\nScope here is therefore **protocol plumbing only**:\n\n1. MCP server scaffolding + stdio transport\n2. Tool registration with JSONSchema params derived from the same payload types used in beats-725327\n3. Identity handling (how \"who did this\" is recorded — see scope note below)\n4. Packaging as a subcommand\n5. Documentation and example client config\n\n## Proposal\n\nAdd a `beats mcp` subcommand that runs an MCP server (stdio transport for local use; HTTP/SSE later only if multi-user use cases emerge) exposing the core operations as structured tools:\n\n- `beats_list` — list issues with filters\n- `beats_show` — fetch an issue with comments and history\n- `beats_add` — wraps `CreateIssue` from [[beats-725327]]\n- `beats_update` — wraps `UpdateIssue`\n- `beats_comment` — wraps `AddComment`\n- `beats_start` / `beats_done` / `beats_planned` / `beats_blocked` — status transitions (thin wrappers, or just `beats_update` with `status` set)\n- `beats_link` — add dependencies/relationships\n- `beats_history` — audit trail\n\nJSONSchema for each tool is derived from the same payload struct used by the JSON-stdin path, so the contract is identical across CLI and MCP.\n\n## Scope decisions to make\n\n- **Transport:** stdio first (per-project local server); HTTP later if needed\n- **Identity:** how the MCP server records \"who\" did the action — config field, client-supplied via tool param, or env var read at server start? (May warrant a separate ticket.)\n- **Distribution:** ship as a subcommand of the existing `beats` binary, no separate process or package\n- **Library reuse:** tools MUST call the internal Go functions from [[beats-725327]] — no duplicated logic, no shelling out\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] `beats mcp` starts an MCP server over stdio\n- [ ] All core read/write operations exposed as tools with JSONSchema params generated from the payload structs in [[beats-725327]]\n- [ ] No business logic duplicated from the CLI path — tools are thin adapters\n- [ ] Example client config snippet documented for Claude Code (`.mcp.json`) and Cursor\n- [ ] Agent identity is recorded on writes\n- [ ] Integration test exercises a full create → comment → done flow over MCP\n\n## Possible breakdown\n\nLikely warrants promotion to an epic once [[beats-725327]] lands, with sub-issues for:\n\n1. MCP server scaffolding + stdio transport\n2. Read-operation tool surface\n3. Write-operation tool surface\n4. Identity/auth handling\n5. Documentation and example client config\n\n## Rationale\n\nMCP is the agent-native answer to [[beats-7ec710]]. By the time this ticket starts, [[beats-725327]] should have already factored business logic into reusable internal functions, so this becomes a focused transport-plumbing effort rather than a re-implementation."},"created_at":"2026-05-27T06:42:52.220148014Z","created_by":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e"}
