{"id":"beats-8c2171","type":"UPDATE","payload":{"status":"DOING"},"created_at":"2026-06-08T07:06:23.782393719Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.161 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e"}
{"id":"beats-8c2171","type":"COMMENT","payload":{"id":"cmt-54693403-bb67-467a-a8e2-b04224745ce7","text":"## Summary\n\nImplemented auto-unparent: when a parent issue is deleted, all children have their `ParentID` cleared automatically.\n\n**What changed:**\n\n- `internal/beats/delete.go` — after appending the DELETE event, `DeleteIssue` now iterates all issues in the projected state, finds children whose `ParentID` matches the deleted issue, and emits UPDATE events to clear their `ParentID`.\n- `internal/beats/beats_test.go` — added `TestDeleteParentUnparentsChildren` covering the case of deleting a parent with two children and verifying both are unparented.\n\n**Rationale:** This is the safe default — no silent data loss. The alternative (cascade-delete children) is a separate, more dangerous feature that should be offered as a user choice in the Web UI, tracked as follow-up work."},"created_at":"2026-06-08T07:58:15.173529044Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.161 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e"}
{"id":"beats-8c2171","type":"COMMENT","payload":{"id":"cmt-f7dfd3c7-b215-4102-b150-de0ab681f851","text":"## Cascade delete — implementation complete\n\n### Backend\n\n- `internal/model/types.go` — added `Cascade bool` to `DeletePayload`\n- `internal/beats/delete.go` — `DeleteIssue` now accepts a variadic `cascade` parameter. When true, children get DELETE events instead of unparent UPDATE events. The cascade reason references the parent ID for traceability.\n- `internal/server/handlers.go` — passes `p.Cascade` from the API request through to `DeleteIssue`\n- `internal/beats/beats_test.go` — added `TestDeleteParentCascadeDeletesChildren` verifying parent + both children are deleted while unrelated issues survive\n\n### Frontend\n\n- `web/src/components/IssueDetail/PropertySidebar.tsx` — when deleting an issue with children, shows a three-option dialog: \"Keep sub-issues\" (unparent), \"Delete sub-issues too\" (cascade), and \"Cancel\". Issues without children get the existing simple confirmation.\n- `web/src/components/ui/ContextMenu.tsx` — same logic for the right-click context menu and the Ctrl+Backspace keyboard shortcut.\n\n### Design rationale\n\n\"Keep sub-issues\" is the prominent (filled) button since it's the safer default. \"Delete sub-issues too\" uses an outlined style to signal the destructive choice. Both paths go through the same `addDraft` → `handleDraft` → `DeleteIssue` pipeline."},"created_at":"2026-06-08T08:13:37.059753978Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.161 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e"}
{"id":"beats-8c2171","type":"UPDATE","payload":{"status":"DONE"},"created_at":"2026-06-08T09:39:09.380136137Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.161 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e"}
{"id":"beats-3a219e","type":"CREATE","payload":{"description":"## Problem\n\nThe projection currently depends on event order in the JSONL file. Git merges can scramble line order — two clones that have seen the same set of events may compute different state depending on which order the lines ended up in after a merge. This breaks the convergence guarantee.\n\n## Solution\n\nEvents carry a **deterministic sort key** — `(timestamp, actor_id, event_id)` — and replay sorts by that key, not by file position.\n\nThen any two clones that have seen the same set of events compute the identical state. That's the convergence guarantee, and it's a pure function — no negotiation, no conflict resolution protocol.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] Define a deterministic sort key: `(timestamp, actor_id, event_id)`\n- [ ] Projection replay sorts events by the sort key before applying them\n- [ ] Two JSONL files with the same events in different line order produce identical projected state\n- [ ] Existing events already have the fields needed for the sort key (verify, backfill if needed)\n- [ ] Add tests: shuffle event order, assert identical projection output","labels":["bug"],"status":"BACKLOG","title":"Replay has to be order independent"},"created_at":"2026-06-08T12:15:02.869112365Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.168 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e"}
{"id":"beats-c762f7","type":"CREATE","payload":{"dependencies":[{"kind":"relates_to","source_id":"beats-c762f7","target_id":"beats-3a219e"}],"description":"## Problem\n\nWhen two branches append events to `.beats/issues.db` concurrently, git produces conflict markers on merge. The file is append-only JSONL — both sides' lines are valid and should both survive — but git's default merge strategy doesn't know that.\n\n## Solution\n\nSet `merge=union` in `.gitattributes`:\n\n```\n.beats/issues.db merge=union\n```\n\nThis tells git to keep lines from both sides on a merge, which is exactly the right semantic for an append-only event log. No conflict markers, no manual resolution.\n\n### Alternative: per-event files\n\nStructurally impossible conflicts — each event is its own file (e.g. `.beats/events/\u003cevent-id\u003e.json`). Adding a file never conflicts with adding a different file. Trade-off: more filesystem overhead, changes to the storage layer. Worth considering long-term, but `merge=union` is the smaller change and solves the immediate problem.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `beats init` creates/updates `.gitattributes` with the `merge=union` rule for `issues.db`\n- [ ] Existing projects pick up the rule via `beats doctor` or `beats migrate`\n- [ ] Verify: two branches that each append events merge cleanly without conflict markers\n- [ ] Document the per-event-files alternative for future consideration\n\n## Related\n\nPairs with **beats-3a219e** (order-independent replay) — union merge can interleave lines in any order, so replay must not depend on file position.","labels":["feature"],"status":"BACKLOG","title":"Non-blocking merge for issues.db via gitattributes"},"created_at":"2026-06-08T12:43:54.041100155Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.168 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e"}
{"id":"beats-f23cf5","type":"CREATE","payload":{"description":"## Overview\n\nExpand the event-sourced projection to use the **right merge type per field**, instead of naïve last-writer-wins on everything. The event log is already append-only and immutable — the change is in how projection folds events into state, and in adding new fine-grained event types for set-valued fields.\n\n## Field → Merge Strategy\n\n| Field | Merge type | Mechanism |\n|---|---|---|\n| `status` | LWW register | Sort-key tiebreak; state machine guards terminal states |\n| `assignee`, `priority`, `points`, `estimate` | LWW register | Conflicts rare, low-stakes |\n| `labels` | OR-Set | New `LABEL_ADD` / `LABEL_REMOVE` event types |\n| `comments` | Grow-only log | Already correct — no change needed |\n| `dependencies` / `links` | OR-Set | New `LINK_ADD` / `LINK_REMOVE` event types |\n| `description` | LWW register | Loser preserved in history; no collaborative-text CRDT |\n| `sort_order` | Fractional indexing | Already uses `GenerateNKeysBetween`; add actor tiebreak |\n\n## Design Decision: New Event Types\n\nNew event types (`LABEL_ADD`, `LABEL_REMOVE`, `LINK_ADD`, `LINK_REMOVE`) rather than delta fields on `UPDATE`. This gives us:\n\n- **Backwards compatibility for free** — old `UPDATE` events with full `labels`/`dependencies` arrays still project correctly as \"set to this value\"; new event types layer on top with finer-grained semantics.\n- **Precise facts in the log** — \"add label bug\" is a better historical record than \"set labels to [feature, bug]\".\n- **Trivial OR-Set projection** — maintain a set of `(value, add-timestamp)` pairs; a `REMOVE` with a later timestamp tombstones the entry.\n\n## Prerequisites\n\n- **beats-3a219e** — Deterministic sort-key replay (without this, \"last writer\" is JSONL line order, which git merge can scramble)\n- **beats-c762f7** — `merge=union` in `.gitattributes` (both sides' events must survive the merge)\n\n## Scope Boundary\n\n- No collaborative-text CRDT (Yjs/Automerge) for `description` — concurrent prose edits are rare and low-stakes\n- No vector clocks or Lamport clocks — wall-clock timestamp + actor + event-id sort key is sufficient for this use case\n- No wire protocol or sync server — git remains the transport","labels":["epic"],"status":"BACKLOG","title":"Per-field CRDT merge semantics"},"created_at":"2026-06-09T06:12:02.043418105Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.169 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e"}
{"id":"beats-3a219e","type":"UPDATE","payload":{"parent_id":"beats-f23cf5","status":"PLANNED"},"created_at":"2026-06-09T06:15:07.011788554Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.169 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e"}
{"id":"beats-c762f7","type":"UPDATE","payload":{"parent_id":"beats-f23cf5","status":"PLANNED"},"created_at":"2026-06-09T06:15:07.011788554Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.169 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e"}
{"id":"beats-16e586","type":"CREATE","payload":{"dependencies":[{"kind":"depends_on","source_id":"beats-16e586","target_id":"beats-3a219e"}],"description":"## Problem\n\nLabels are stored as a full `[]string` replacement in `UpdatePayload`. If Alice adds \"bug\" (setting labels to `[\"feature\", \"bug\"]`) and Bob adds \"urgent\" (setting labels to `[\"feature\", \"urgent\"]`) concurrently, after merge one addition is silently lost — the last-writer's full array wins.\n\n## Solution\n\nIntroduce two new event types:\n\n- **`LABEL_ADD`** — payload: `{ label: string }`\n- **`LABEL_REMOVE`** — payload: `{ label: string }`\n\n### Projection semantics (OR-Set)\n\nFor each issue, maintain a map of `label → { added_at, removed_at }`. During replay (events sorted by deterministic sort key):\n\n- `LABEL_ADD` sets `added_at = event.sort_key` if later than current `added_at`\n- `LABEL_REMOVE` sets `removed_at = event.sort_key` if later than current `removed_at`\n- A label is **present** when `added_at \u003e removed_at` (or `removed_at` is zero)\n\nThis means concurrent \"add bug\" and \"add urgent\" both survive. A remove only wins against adds that happened before it.\n\n### Backwards compatibility\n\nOld `UPDATE` events with a `labels` field continue to work: the projection treats them as a \"set to exactly this list\" — implicitly adding labels that appear and removing labels that don't, all at the event's sort key. New `LABEL_ADD`/`LABEL_REMOVE` events with later timestamps layer on top correctly.\n\n### CLI / MCP surface\n\n- `beats update --add-label bug` → emits `LABEL_ADD`\n- `beats update --remove-label bug` → emits `LABEL_REMOVE`\n- `beats update --labels \"bug,urgent\"` (full replacement) → still emits an `UPDATE` event for backwards compat, or emits the equivalent add/remove deltas (design choice)\n- MCP tools: add `add_labels` / `remove_labels` parameters to `beats_update`\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] Define `LABEL_ADD` and `LABEL_REMOVE` event types and payloads in `internal/model/types.go`\n- [ ] Update projection in `internal/beats/projection.go` to handle OR-Set semantics for labels\n- [ ] Old `UPDATE` events with `labels` field still project correctly\n- [ ] CLI flags `--add-label` and `--remove-label`\n- [ ] MCP tool parameters `add_labels` / `remove_labels`\n- [ ] Test: concurrent add of different labels both survive after merge\n- [ ] Test: remove-before-add (add wins); add-before-remove (remove wins)","estimate":5,"labels":["feature"],"parent_id":"beats-f23cf5","status":"BACKLOG","title":"Labels OR-Set: LABEL_ADD / LABEL_REMOVE event types"},"created_at":"2026-06-09T06:12:21.974171215Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.169 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e"}
{"id":"beats-f847da","type":"CREATE","payload":{"dependencies":[{"kind":"depends_on","source_id":"beats-f847da","target_id":"beats-3a219e"}],"description":"## Problem\n\nDependencies/links suffer the same full-replacement problem as labels. `UpdatePayload.Dependencies` is `[]Dependency` — a full overwrite. Concurrent \"add blocks B\" and \"add relates_to C\" will clobber one.\n\n## Solution\n\nIntroduce two new event types:\n\n- **`LINK_ADD`** — payload: `{ target_id: string, kind: string }` (kind = blocks, depends_on, relates_to, etc.)\n- **`LINK_REMOVE`** — payload: `{ target_id: string, kind: string }`\n\n### Projection semantics (OR-Set)\n\nSame pattern as labels: maintain a map of `(target_id, kind) → { added_at, removed_at }`. A link is present when `added_at \u003e removed_at`.\n\n### Backwards compatibility\n\nOld `UPDATE` events with a `dependencies` field treated as full replacement at the event's sort key. Old `CREATE` events with initial dependencies treated as `LINK_ADD` at creation time. New `LINK_ADD`/`LINK_REMOVE` events layer on top.\n\n### Existing `beats_link` tool\n\n`beats_link` already exists as a dedicated MCP tool. Internally it should now emit a `LINK_ADD` event instead of an `UPDATE` with the full dependency list. Similarly, a future `beats_unlink` tool emits `LINK_REMOVE`.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] Define `LINK_ADD` and `LINK_REMOVE` event types and payloads in `internal/model/types.go`\n- [ ] Update projection to handle OR-Set semantics for links\n- [ ] Old `UPDATE` events with `dependencies` and old `CREATE` events with initial deps still project correctly\n- [ ] `beats_link` MCP tool emits `LINK_ADD` event\n- [ ] New `beats_unlink` MCP tool / CLI command emits `LINK_REMOVE`\n- [ ] Test: concurrent link additions both survive\n- [ ] Test: unlink then re-link semantics","estimate":5,"labels":["feature"],"parent_id":"beats-f23cf5","status":"BACKLOG","title":"Links OR-Set: LINK_ADD / LINK_REMOVE event types"},"created_at":"2026-06-09T06:12:31.456247219Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.169 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e"}
{"id":"beats-267e29","type":"CREATE","payload":{"dependencies":[{"kind":"depends_on","source_id":"beats-267e29","target_id":"beats-3a219e"}],"description":"## Problem\n\nConcurrent status transitions (e.g., one actor sets `DONE`, another sets `BLOCKED`) currently resolve by file order. After beats-3a219e lands, they'll resolve by sort key — but the projection should also enforce the state machine so invalid transitions from stale actors don't corrupt state.\n\n## Solution\n\nDuring projection, when two `UPDATE` events set `status` with the same issue:\n\n1. Sort by deterministic sort key (timestamp, actor, event_id) — already guaranteed by beats-3a219e.\n2. Apply in order. The last valid transition wins.\n3. **Terminal state guard**: if an issue reaches `DONE`, a concurrent in-flight `UPDATE` to `DOING` from a stale actor (earlier sort key) has already been applied and overwritten. If it has a *later* sort key, it reopens the issue — which is correct (the later actor had more recent intent).\n4. The \"losing\" status transition is still visible in `beats_history` for auditability.\n\n### Concurrent DONE vs BLOCKED\n\nBoth are valid end-states for different reasons. Sort-key ordering applies: the later one wins. The earlier transition is preserved in the event log and visible via `beats_history`.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] Projection applies status updates in sort-key order (not file order)\n- [ ] State machine validation: invalid transitions (e.g., `DONE` → `BACKLOG`) are skipped with a warning in history\n- [ ] `beats_history` shows superseded status transitions\n- [ ] Test: concurrent DONE vs BLOCKED resolves by sort key\n- [ ] Test: stale actor's transition applied then overwritten by later actor","estimate":3,"labels":["feature"],"parent_id":"beats-f23cf5","status":"BACKLOG","title":"Status LWW with state-machine guard and sort-key tiebreak"},"created_at":"2026-06-09T06:12:43.340818565Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.169 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e"}
{"id":"beats-4fa8dd","type":"CREATE","payload":{"dependencies":[{"kind":"depends_on","source_id":"beats-4fa8dd","target_id":"beats-3a219e"}],"description":"## Problem\n\nConcurrent description edits are rare and low-stakes, but the losing edit currently vanishes silently. The winner should stick (LWW by sort key), but the loser should be visible in the audit trail.\n\n## Solution\n\nNo change to the projection logic — description remains LWW by sort key (once beats-3a219e lands). The change is in **presentation**:\n\n- `beats_history` should show when a description update was superseded by a concurrent edit with a later sort key.\n- The superseded description text should be viewable (e.g., `beats history \u003cid\u003e` shows the full text of the losing edit).\n\n**Explicitly out of scope**: collaborative-text CRDT (Yjs/Automerge). The machinery would dwarf the rest of the tool for negligible benefit.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] Description projection uses sort-key ordering (free once beats-3a219e lands)\n- [ ] `beats_history` flags superseded description edits\n- [ ] Superseded description text is viewable in history output\n- [ ] Test: concurrent description edits, later sort key wins, earlier visible in history","estimate":2,"labels":["feature"],"parent_id":"beats-f23cf5","status":"BACKLOG","title":"Description LWW with loser preserved in history"},"created_at":"2026-06-09T06:12:50.897599769Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.169 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e"}
{"id":"beats-730ec2","type":"CREATE","payload":{"dependencies":[{"kind":"depends_on","source_id":"beats-730ec2","target_id":"beats-3a219e"}],"description":"## Problem\n\n`sort_order` already uses fractional indexing via `sortorder.GenerateNKeysBetween`. But if two actors concurrently insert an item between the same two neighbors, they'll generate the same fractional key. There's no tiebreak — the items end up in arbitrary order.\n\n## Solution\n\nWhen two `sort_order` values are identical, tiebreak by `actor_id` (lexicographic). This is deterministic and consistent across clones.\n\nThe probability of collision is low (fractional indexing has a large key space), but when it happens, the tiebreak ensures convergence rather than non-determinism.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] Projection tiebreaks identical `sort_order` values by actor ID\n- [ ] Test: two events set same `sort_order`, deterministic ordering by actor\n- [ ] Verify existing fractional indexing logic handles the edge case","estimate":1,"labels":["feature"],"parent_id":"beats-f23cf5","status":"BACKLOG","title":"Sort-order fractional indexing: actor tiebreak"},"created_at":"2026-06-09T06:12:56.921608897Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.169 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e"}
