{"id":"beats-625960","type":"UPDATE","payload":{"description":"Create a landing page for beats. The landing page should cover:\n\n- Philosophy (why beats, git native issues vs SaaS)\n- Who beats is for (solo, small teams working together with AI agents)\n- Who beats is not for (large teams, live updates)\n- Quickstart\n- Installation\n- Usage (Overview, Issues, Board, Cycles, Labels, Dependencies, Config, Starting Branches, Merging Branches, MCP, Multi-Project)\n- Architecture\n\nThe landing page should cover that beats is open-source and available for free with a link to the GitHub repo."},"created_at":"2026-06-09T16:17:50.452279752Z","created_by":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e"}
{"id":"beats-075586","type":"UPDATE","payload":{"title":"Distributed Mode: self-hosted beats server with SSH key auth","description":"## Vision\n\nSame `beats` binary, two modes. **Local mode** (default): reads `.beats/issues.db` directly, projects in memory. **Distributed mode** (after `beats login`): CLI and web UI transparently proxy to a remote beats server via REST API. No workflow change for developers — same commands, same MCP tools, same web UI.\n\n## Architecture\n\n### Server: `beats serve`\n\nThe beats binary itself, running as a headless HTTP server. Single port exposes:\n\n- **REST API** (`/api/*`) — same endpoints the web UI uses today. CLI and web UI both consume this.\n- **MCP endpoint** (`/mcp`) — same tool surface as `beats mcp`, over HTTP with bearer auth. Agents point their MCP config here directly.\n- **No static assets** — the server is headless. The web UI is served locally by `beats board`.\n\nDeployment: minimal Alpine Docker image with the beats binary. `.beats/` directory host-mounted containing `config.yaml`, `authorized_keys`, and `issues.db`.\n\n```dockerfile\nFROM alpine:latest\nCOPY beats /usr/local/bin/beats\nEXPOSE 8080\nENTRYPOINT [\"beats\", \"serve\"]\n```\n\nConcurrency: write mutex on event appends. The event log is append-only so serialized writes are cheap and correct.\n\n### Authentication: SSH keys → JWT bearer tokens\n\nUsers authenticate by proving ownership of an SSH private key. Identity comes from `.beats/authorized_keys`:\n\n```\nssh-ed25519 AAAAC3... alice@team.dev\nssh-ed25519 AAAAC3... bob@team.dev\n```\n\nThe public key is the credential, the comment field is the identity (maps to `Event.CreatedBy`).\n\n**Login flow:**\n\n1. `beats login https://beats.internal`\n2. Server returns a nonce\n3. CLI signs the nonce with the user's SSH key\n4. Server verifies signature against `.beats/authorized_keys`\n5. Server returns a JWT containing the email identity + expiry\n6. Token cached in project config (gitignored)\n\n**Token usage:**\n\n- CLI: `Authorization: Bearer \u003cjwt\u003e` on every REST call\n- MCP agents: same header in MCP client config\n- Web UI: the local `beats board` proxy injects the token — browser never sees it\n\n### Client transport abstraction\n\nThe `Client` struct gets a transport layer:\n\n```\nLocal mode:   CLI → Client → storage.ReadEvents() → ProjectIssues()\nRemote mode:  CLI → Client → HTTP GET /api/issues → JSON response\n```\n\nEvery CLI command and MCP tool handler calls the same `Client` methods. Only the transport changes. Detection is automatic: if the project config has a remote URL + token, use HTTP transport.\n\n### Web UI: local proxy\n\n`beats board` in remote mode starts a local web server serving the embedded frontend, but proxies `/api/*` to the remote server with the bearer token injected.\n\n```\nBrowser → localhost:8080/api/issues → proxy → https://beats.internal/api/issues\n                                       ↑ injects Authorization header\n```\n\nSame UX as local mode. No CORS issues. Remote doesn't need to be browser-accessible.\n\n### Real-time: SSE (future)\n\n`GET /api/events` as a Server-Sent Events stream. The local proxy forwards it. Live updates when another team member changes an issue. Not v1 but the architecture should leave room for it.\n\n## Scope boundary\n\n- **No CRDT / distributed merge** — the server is the single source of truth. Local-first with git sync is the solo mode; distributed mode is centralized.\n- **No git auto-backup in v1** — the server's `.beats/` directory is the canonical state. Backups are an ops concern (volume snapshots, cron rsync, etc.).\n- **No TLS termination in v1** — run behind a reverse proxy (nginx, caddy, traefik) for TLS. Standard practice for Docker deployments.\n- **REST over gRPC** — simpler toolchain, browser-compatible, debuggable with curl. gRPC's strengths (high throughput, bidirectional streaming) don't apply to an issue tracker's scale.\"","status":"PLANNED","sort_order":"aF"},"created_at":"2026-06-10T12:59:34.720962408Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.169 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e"}
{"id":"beats-c5c768","type":"CREATE","payload":{"description":"## Summary\n\nIntroduce a transport layer in the `Client` struct so that every CLI command and MCP tool handler works transparently in both local and remote mode.\n\n## Design\n\nDefine a `Transport` interface:\n\n```go\ntype Transport interface {\n    ListIssues(opts FilterOptions) ([]*model.Issue, error)\n    GetIssue(id string) (*model.Issue, error)\n    UpdateIssue(id string, payload model.UpdatePayload, action string) ([]string, error)\n    AddIssue(payload model.CreatePayload) (*model.Issue, error)\n    AddComment(issueID, text string) error\n    // ... all operations the Client currently exposes\n}\n```\n\nTwo implementations:\n\n- **`LocalTransport`** — wraps the current `storage.ReadEvents()` + `ProjectIssues()` logic. This is what exists today, just extracted behind the interface.\n- **`RemoteTransport`** — HTTP client that calls `GET /api/issues`, `POST /api/draft`, etc. with the bearer token from config.\n\n`NewClient(cfg)` checks `cfg.RemoteURL` — if set (and token present), uses `RemoteTransport`. Otherwise `LocalTransport`.\n\n## Key decisions\n\n- The interface boundary is at the operation level (list, get, update), NOT at the storage level (read events, append event). Remote mode doesn't expose the event log — it talks REST.\n- `RemoteTransport` reuses the same REST endpoints the web UI already consumes. No new API surface.\n- Branch-related operations (review, merge, branch stats) remain local-only — they need the git working tree. These should gracefully degrade or error in remote mode.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `Transport` interface defined\n- [ ] `LocalTransport` extracted from current `Client` methods\n- [ ] `RemoteTransport` implementing the interface via HTTP\n- [ ] `NewClient` auto-selects transport based on config\n- [ ] All existing CLI commands work unchanged in local mode\n- [ ] CLI commands work against a running server in remote mode\n- [ ] MCP tool handlers work in both modes","estimate":8,"labels":["feature"],"parent_id":"beats-075586","status":"BACKLOG","title":"Client transport abstraction: local vs. remote"},"created_at":"2026-06-10T12:33:04.226950433Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.169 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e"}
{"id":"beats-834350","type":"CREATE","payload":{"description":"## Summary\n\nNew `beats serve` subcommand that starts a headless HTTP server exposing the REST API and MCP endpoint, both behind bearer token auth.\n\n## Design\n\nSingle port (default `0.0.0.0:8080`, configurable via `--addr` flag) serves:\n\n- `POST /auth/challenge` — returns a nonce for SSH key auth\n- `POST /auth/verify` — accepts signed nonce, returns JWT\n- `/api/*` — existing REST API endpoints, auth middleware required\n- `/mcp` — MCP-over-HTTP endpoint, auth middleware required\n\nAuth middleware extracts the bearer token from `Authorization` header, verifies the JWT signature and expiry, and injects the user identity into the request context.\n\nWrite concurrency: a `sync.Mutex` serializes all event appends. The event log is append-only so this is cheap.\n\n## Server identity\n\nThe server reads `.beats/authorized_keys` for user verification. It generates a signing key on first run (stored in `.beats/server.key`) for JWT issuance.\n\n## Deployment\n\n```dockerfile\nFROM alpine:latest\nCOPY beats /usr/local/bin/beats\nEXPOSE 8080\nENTRYPOINT [\"beats\", \"serve\"]\n```\n\nHost-mount `.beats/` containing `config.yaml`, `authorized_keys`, `issues.db`.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `beats serve` starts HTTP server on configurable address\n- [ ] REST API endpoints served with auth middleware\n- [ ] MCP endpoint served with auth middleware\n- [ ] Write mutex on event appends\n- [ ] Server reads `.beats/authorized_keys` for user verification\n- [ ] JWT signing key generated and persisted\n- [ ] Graceful shutdown on SIGTERM\n- [ ] Health check endpoint (`GET /healthz`, no auth)","estimate":8,"labels":["feature"],"parent_id":"beats-075586","status":"BACKLOG","title":"beats serve: headless HTTP server"},"created_at":"2026-06-10T12:33:13.00426524Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.169 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e"}
{"id":"beats-23dc97","type":"CREATE","payload":{"description":"## Summary\n\nImplement the SSH key authentication flow: server issues a nonce, client signs it with their SSH private key, server verifies against `authorized_keys` and returns a JWT.\n\n## Auth flow\n\n1. Client: `POST /auth/challenge` → server returns `{ nonce: \"\u003crandom-bytes-base64\u003e\" }`\n2. Client signs the nonce using the SSH private key (via `golang.org/x/crypto/ssh`)\n3. Client: `POST /auth/verify` with `{ public_key: \"\u003cbase64\u003e\", signature: \"\u003cbase64\u003e\", nonce: \"\u003cnonce\u003e\" }`\n4. Server verifies:\n   - Nonce is valid and not expired (5 min TTL)\n   - Public key exists in `.beats/authorized_keys`\n   - Signature is valid for the nonce\n5. Server returns `{ token: \"\u003cjwt\u003e\", expires_at: \"\u003ctimestamp\u003e\" }`\n\n## JWT\n\n- Signed with the server's private key (`.beats/server.key`, ed25519)\n- Claims: `sub` (email from authorized_keys comment), `exp` (7 days), `iat`\n- Stateless verification — no token database\n\n## authorized_keys format\n\nStandard SSH `authorized_keys` format. The comment field is the user identity:\n\n```\nssh-ed25519 AAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5... alice@team.dev\nssh-rsa AAAAB3NzaC1yc2EAAAA... bob@team.dev\n```\n\nIdentity maps to `Event.CreatedBy` as `alice \u003calice@team.dev\u003e`.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `POST /auth/challenge` returns a time-limited nonce\n- [ ] `POST /auth/verify` validates signature and returns JWT\n- [ ] Server parses `.beats/authorized_keys` file\n- [ ] JWT contains user identity from key comment\n- [ ] JWT expiry configurable (default 7 days)\n- [ ] Expired/invalid tokens rejected by auth middleware\n- [ ] Nonce replay prevention (single-use)","estimate":5,"labels":["feature"],"parent_id":"beats-075586","status":"BACKLOG","title":"SSH key auth: challenge-response and JWT issuance"},"created_at":"2026-06-10T12:33:22.363312566Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.169 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e"}
{"id":"beats-72811f","type":"CREATE","payload":{"description":"## Summary\n\nNew `beats login \u003cserver-url\u003e` command that authenticates against a remote beats server using SSH keys and saves the token for subsequent use.\n\n## Flow\n\n1. `beats login https://beats.internal`\n2. CLI detects available SSH keys (checks `~/.ssh/id_ed25519`, `~/.ssh/id_rsa`, ssh-agent)\n3. If multiple keys, prompt user to select\n4. Requests a challenge nonce from the server\n5. Signs the nonce with the selected SSH key\n6. Sends the signed response to the server\n7. Receives and caches the JWT in `.beats/config.yaml` under `remote_url` and `remote_token`\n8. Confirms: \"Logged in to https://beats.internal as alice@team.dev\"\n\n## Config storage\n\n```yaml\nremote_url: https://beats.internal\nremote_token: eyJhbGciOiJFZDI1NTE5...\n```\n\nThese fields should be in `.beats/config.yaml` but also in `.gitignore` — or better, stored in a separate `.beats/remote.yaml` that's gitignored by default.\n\n## Additional commands\n\n- `beats logout` — removes the cached token\n- `beats whoami` — displays current identity and server URL (calls a `/api/user` or decodes the JWT locally)\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `beats login \u003curl\u003e` authenticates and stores token\n- [ ] Auto-detects SSH keys from standard locations + ssh-agent\n- [ ] Prompts for key selection when multiple available\n- [ ] Token stored in gitignored config\n- [ ] `beats logout` clears token\n- [ ] `beats whoami` shows identity and server\n- [ ] Error handling: server unreachable, key not authorized, invalid signature","estimate":5,"labels":["feature","CLI"],"parent_id":"beats-075586","status":"BACKLOG","title":"beats login: CLI authentication command"},"created_at":"2026-06-10T12:33:31.365411942Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.169 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e"}
{"id":"beats-703976","type":"CREATE","payload":{"description":"## Summary\n\nWhen a remote server is configured, `beats board` starts the local web server with the embedded frontend as usual, but proxies all `/api/*` requests to the remote server with the bearer token injected.\n\n## Design\n\nThe existing `beats board` starts an HTTP server that serves static assets and handles API routes by reading the local event log. In remote mode:\n\n- Static assets (`/`, `/assets/*`) still served locally from the embedded frontend\n- `/api/*` routes are reverse-proxied to the remote server\n- The proxy adds `Authorization: Bearer \u003ctoken\u003e` to every forwarded request\n- The frontend code is unchanged — it always talks to `localhost`\n\nDetection: if `cfg.RemoteURL` is set, the server uses proxy handlers instead of local handlers for `/api/*` routes.\n\n## Considerations\n\n- Proxy should forward SSE streams when we add real-time events (future)\n- Timeout handling: proxy requests should have a reasonable timeout (30s)\n- Error handling: if the remote is unreachable, return a clear error to the frontend\n- The `/mcp` endpoint on the local server is not proxied — agents should point directly at the remote MCP endpoint\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `beats board` detects remote config and enables proxy mode\n- [ ] `/api/*` routes proxied to remote with auth header\n- [ ] Static assets served locally\n- [ ] Frontend works identically in both modes\n- [ ] Proxy timeout and error handling\n- [ ] Clear error message when remote is unreachable","estimate":3,"labels":["feature","WebApp"],"parent_id":"beats-075586","status":"BACKLOG","title":"beats board: remote proxy mode"},"created_at":"2026-06-10T12:33:40.746359478Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.169 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e"}
{"id":"beats-24db09","type":"CREATE","payload":{"description":"## Summary\n\nCreate a Dockerfile and deployment documentation for running beats in distributed mode.\n\n## Deliverables\n\n- **Dockerfile** — minimal Alpine image with the beats binary, `EXPOSE 8080`, entrypoint `beats serve`\n- **docker-compose.yml** — example compose file with `.beats/` volume mount\n- **Setup guide** — how to initialize `.beats/authorized_keys`, configure the server, and have developers run `beats login`\n\n## Docker image\n\n```dockerfile\nFROM alpine:latest\nCOPY beats /usr/local/bin/beats\nEXPOSE 8080\nENTRYPOINT [\"beats\", \"serve\"]\n```\n\n## Example compose\n\n```yaml\nservices:\n  beats:\n    image: beats:latest\n    ports:\n      - \"8080:8080\"\n    volumes:\n      - ./beats-data:/data/.beats\n    command: [\"beats\", \"serve\", \"--data-dir\", \"/data/.beats\"]\n```\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] Dockerfile builds and produces a working image\n- [ ] docker-compose.yml starts the server with persistent storage\n- [ ] `.beats/authorized_keys` can be managed via volume mount\n- [ ] Makefile target `make docker` builds the image\n- [ ] Basic setup documentation","estimate":3,"labels":["feature"],"parent_id":"beats-075586","status":"BACKLOG","title":"Docker image and deployment packaging"},"created_at":"2026-06-10T12:33:46.467460838Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.169 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e"}
