{"id":"beats-46d0d2","type":"CREATE","payload":{"title":"Issue locking: prevent claim races on beats start","description":"## Summary\n\nPrevent two agents or developers from simultaneously claiming the same issue. `beats start` should fail if the issue is already DOING or already has an active branch.\n\n## Problem\n\nTwo agents see a PLANNED issue, both call `beats start` at the same time. Both succeed, both create branches, both do duplicate work. In local mode this is a read-check-write race with no atomicity. In distributed mode the write mutex serializes events but the check-then-act is still non-atomic.\n\n## Design\n\n### Server (distributed mode)\n\nAtomic check-and-transition: within the write mutex, verify the issue is still PLANNED/BACKLOG before transitioning to DOING. If another request already transitioned it, return an error. The mutex makes this naturally serializable.\n\n### Local mode\n\nOptimistic locking: before writing the DOING event, re-read and re-project to verify the issue hasn't been transitioned. This doesn't prevent all races (two processes between read and write) but covers the common case.\n\n### Branch detection guard\n\n`beats start` should also check if a branch already exists for the issue (local or remote) and refuse to create a duplicate. This catches the case where someone started work but didn't update the status.\n\n### CLI/MCP behavior\n\n- `beats start` on an already-DOING issue: error with \"Issue is already in progress (assigned to X)\"\n- `beats start` on an issue with an existing branch: error with \"Branch already exists: \u003cbranch-name\u003e\"\n- Both should suggest `beats start --force` if the user really wants to take over\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `beats start` rejects already-DOING issues with clear error message\n- [ ] `beats start` rejects issues with existing branches\n- [ ] Server mode: atomic check-and-transition within write mutex\n- [ ] Local mode: optimistic re-check before write\n- [ ] `--force` flag to override (reassign to self)\n- [ ] Error messages include current assignee and branch name","status":"BACKLOG","parent_id":"beats-075586","estimate":3,"labels":["feature"]},"created_at":"2026-06-10T15:38:02.245599981Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.169 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e"}
{"id":"beats-6c89d2","type":"CREATE","payload":{"title":"Git operation lock for concurrent branch operations","description":"## Summary\n\n`beats start`, `beats merge`, and `beats review` all manipulate the git working tree — checkout branches, run merges, compute diffs. If two agents run git-mutating operations simultaneously, they corrupt each other's state.\n\n## Problem\n\nAgent A runs `beats merge issue-1` (checks out main, merges branch). Agent B runs `beats start issue-2` (creates and checks out a new branch). Both try to change the checked-out branch at the same time. Results are unpredictable — failed checkouts, partial merges, wrong branch context.\n\nThe write mutex on `issues.db` doesn't help — this is about git working tree state, not the event log.\n\n## Design\n\n### File-based lock\n\nUse a lockfile (`.beats/git.lock`) for all git-mutating operations:\n\n- `beats start` (creates/checks out branch)\n- `beats merge` (checks out main, runs merge)\n- Any future operation that changes the working tree\n\nRead-only git operations (`beats review`, branch stats, diff computation) don't need the lock.\n\n### Implementation\n\n```go\nfunc withGitLock(fn func() error) error {\n    lock := flock.New(\".beats/git.lock\")\n    if err := lock.Lock(); err != nil {\n        return fmt.Errorf(\"another beats operation is using git — try again\")\n    }\n    defer lock.Unlock()\n    return fn()\n}\n```\n\nUse `github.com/gofrs/flock` for cross-platform file locking.\n\n### Timeout\n\nLock acquisition should timeout after 30 seconds with a helpful message (\"beats merge is currently running, waiting...\").\n\n### Alternative: git worktrees\n\nFor a more robust solution, git-mutating operations could use temporary worktrees instead of manipulating the main checkout. This is more complex but allows true parallelism. Could be a follow-up.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] File-based lock for git-mutating operations\n- [ ] `beats start` and `beats merge` acquire lock before git operations\n- [ ] Lock timeout with helpful error message\n- [ ] Read-only operations (review, diff, stats) don't acquire lock\n- [ ] Lock released on process crash (flock handles this)\n- [ ] Works on Linux, macOS, Windows","status":"BACKLOG","parent_id":"beats-075586","estimate":3,"labels":["feature"]},"created_at":"2026-06-10T15:38:14.008237541Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.169 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e"}
{"id":"beats-7cd82d","type":"CREATE","payload":{"title":"beats inbox: personal notification feed","description":"## Summary\n\n`beats inbox` shows events relevant to you since your last check — assignments, comments on your issues, status changes, merges. For developers working in the terminal who don't have the board open.\n\n## Design\n\n### CLI command\n\n```\n$ beats inbox\n  2m ago   alice assigned beats-abc123 to you\n  5m ago   bob commented on beats-def456 \"Looks good, one nit...\"\n  1h ago   alice merged beats-ghi789 via squash\n  3h ago   carol changed beats-abc123 status to BLOCKED\n\n3 new since last check. Run `beats inbox --clear` to mark as read.\n```\n\n### What's \"relevant to you\"\n\nEvents on issues where you are:\n- The assignee\n- The creator\n- A commenter (you've participated in the conversation)\n\nPlus all MERGE events (team-wide visibility).\n\n### Read tracking\n\nStore a timestamp in `.beats/inbox.last` (or `remote.yaml` for distributed mode). `beats inbox` shows events after that timestamp. `beats inbox --clear` updates the timestamp to now.\n\n### Distributed mode\n\nIn distributed mode, the server has all events. `beats inbox` calls `GET /api/inbox` which filters events server-side based on the authenticated user's identity.\n\n### Local mode\n\nIn local mode, scan the event log and filter by the git user identity. Less useful (you're the only user) but still handy for tracking what your agents did while you were away.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `beats inbox` shows recent events relevant to the current user\n- [ ] Events filtered by assignee, creator, commenter, plus merges\n- [ ] Timestamp-based read tracking\n- [ ] `beats inbox --clear` marks all as read\n- [ ] Works in local mode (scan event log)\n- [ ] Works in distributed mode (server-side filtering)\n- [ ] Compact, readable terminal output","status":"BACKLOG","parent_id":"beats-075586","estimate":5,"labels":["feature","CLI"]},"created_at":"2026-06-10T15:38:25.549655005Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.169 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e"}
{"id":"beats-3fe8c8","type":"CREATE","payload":{"title":"Role-based permissions via config","description":"## Summary\n\nSupport a permissions model defined in `.beats/config.yaml` that controls what different users can do. Not enforced in local mode (trust model), enforced by the server in distributed mode.\n\n## Design\n\nPermissions defined in config as a simple role → capabilities mapping:\n\n```yaml\npermissions:\n  roles:\n    admin:\n      - \"*\"\n    developer:\n      - issue.create\n      - issue.update\n      - issue.comment\n      - issue.start\n      - issue.merge\n      - issue.link\n    viewer:\n      - issue.read\n      - issue.comment\n\n  users:\n    alice@team.dev: admin\n    bob@team.dev: developer\n    intern@team.dev: viewer\n```\n\n### Capabilities\n\n- `issue.read` — list, show, history\n- `issue.create` — add new issues\n- `issue.update` — change status, labels, assignee, etc.\n- `issue.comment` — add comments\n- `issue.start` — start work (transition to DOING + branch)\n- `issue.merge` — merge branches\n- `issue.delete` — delete issues\n- `issue.link` — add/remove relationships\n- `*` — all capabilities\n\n### Enforcement\n\n- **Local mode**: not enforced. Single user, trust model.\n- **Distributed mode**: server middleware checks the authenticated user's role against the required capability for each endpoint. Returns 403 if denied.\n\n### Default\n\nIf no permissions config exists, all authenticated users have full access (backwards compatible).\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] Config schema for roles and user-role mapping\n- [ ] Server middleware checks capabilities per endpoint\n- [ ] 403 response with clear message on denied operations\n- [ ] Default: full access when no permissions configured\n- [ ] `beats whoami` shows current user's role and capabilities\n- [ ] Admin role cannot be locked out (at least one admin required)","status":"BACKLOG","parent_id":"beats-075586","estimate":5,"labels":["feature"]},"created_at":"2026-06-10T15:38:35.363865927Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.169 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e"}
